WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.380
Good to have you on Planetarian Daily, episode 83,

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.820
coming to you on June 8th, 2026.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.140
Let's get into today's research and health developments.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.820
Acetylcholine surges in the brain

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.540
when expected rewards fail,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.880
prompting mice to switch strategies in a virtual maze.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.140
Researchers tracked mice in a virtual maze

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.280
and observed that the absence of an expected reward

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.400
triggered a rapid increase in acetylcholine release.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.620
Blocking acetylcholine reduced the animals

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.000
willingness to abandon familiar paths

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.400
and explore alternatives.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.500
The findings point to a specific neurotransmitter mechanism

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.700
that supports behavioral flexibility when outcomes change.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.780
The study used direct observation

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.080
of neurotransmitter dynamics during task performance.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.980
Future work will examine whether similar acetylcholine dynamics

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.120
operate in human decision-making under uncertainty.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.540
The acetylcholine findings supply a concrete cellular mechanism

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.940
for updating behavior when expectations are violated.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.720
The same circuitry may relate to disorders marked by behavioral rigidity.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.040
Mapping these dynamics in humans would clarify

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.320
whether the mechanism generalizes beyond rodents.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.660
What behavioral or imaging markers might reveal

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.020
analogous acetylcholine activity

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.040
during human decision-making under changing rewards?

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.980
From one reward-related brain signal

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:31.140
to another dietary factor that also influences long-term brain health.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.160
Most people here in nitrate

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.280
and picture a single harmful compound

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.600
to avoid in water or cured meats.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.120
In reality, the same molecule behaves differently

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.080
depending on its dietary origin.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.500
Right now, as you eat a salad,

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.420
nitrate from leafy greens is converted by oral bacteria

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.640
into nitric oxide that supports vascular function.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.980
The same nitrate swallowed from processed meat

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.080
or contaminated water follows different metabolic routes

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.960
that can form compounds linked to oxidative stress.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.320
A study of more than 54,000 adults

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.860
found vegetable derived nitrate associated

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.660
with lower dementia incidence

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.320
while equivalent amounts from meat or water

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.800
showed the opposite pattern.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.320
The contrast arises because vegetable matrices

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.820
supply antioxidants and fiber

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.760
that altered downstream chemistry.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.580
One practical step is to track where

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.520
nitrate appears in your own diet

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.980
rather than treating all sources as equivalent.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.740
From population-level dietary effects on cognition

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.240
to a different environmental exposure

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.300
that may also shape microbial communities over time.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.500
DNA extracted from dental calculus

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.980
of Edo period and modern Japanese skeletal remains

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.540
showed distinct microbial communities

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.560
across time periods and regions.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.380
Several oral bacterial species

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.460
exhibited phylogenetic differences

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.000
linked to historical dietary or environmental changes.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.920
The work demonstrates that preserved calculus

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.040
can track long-term human microbe co-evolution.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.860
Comparisons between pre-modern and contemporary samples

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.560
highlighted both continuity and divergence

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.260
in microbial composition.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.440
The authors note calculus as a new archive

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.960
for reconstructing population health history.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.680
From long-term microbial records

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.700
to shorter-term environmental pressures

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.460
that still select for resistance.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.120
Experiments showed that antibiotics

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.080
and their degradation products

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.760
continued to select for resistant bacteria

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.180
after wastewater treatment

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.840
and release into rivers and coastal waters.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.780
Resistance genes persisted and spread

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.160
even at concentrations

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.480
below typical detection thresholds

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:43.520
for the parent compounds.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.640
The study marks the first direct demonstration

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.480
of this effect in marine and freshwater settings.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.400
Samples were collected on World Ocean's day

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.360
from multiple discharge sites.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.700
The findings indicate that current treatment standards

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.560
may not fully address resistance selection pressures.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.200
From chemical persistence in water

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.500
to engineered materials

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.800
that deliberately enhance energy flow at small scales.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.380
Engineers constructed gold-based meta-materials

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.220
that increased energy flow across nano-scale gaps

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.380
by up to four times compared with conventional materials.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.100
The structures exploit near-field

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.460
radiative heat transfer

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.520
that conventional theory predicts

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.180
should be limited at these scales.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.700
Measurements confirmed the enhancement

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.820
held across multiple gap distances

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.500
and material configurations.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.940
The result challenges assumptions

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.800
about thermal transport limits

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.560
in miniaturized systems.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.780
Applications under investigation

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.420
include chip-level cooling

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.120
and compact energy devices.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.460
From heat management and devices

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.720
to heat and habitat management on restored land.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:50.780
Field measurements in Germany

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.020
showed that photovoltaic installations

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.500
on restored petalins

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.560
created nesting and foraging habitat

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.140
from multiple bird species.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.440
The combination of re-wedding

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.200
and panel shading reduced greenhouse gas emissions

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:03.920
from the pet

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:05.660
while generating electricity.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.680
Bird surveys recorded higher species

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.780
diversity than undrained agricultural petland.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.840
The study quantified both carbon fluxes

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.360
and avian use across replicated plots.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.700
The land use model is now being evaluated

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:21.880
at additional sites.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.160
From landscape-level interventions

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.060
back to cellular-level signals

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.640
that also guide adaptive behavior.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.060
New measurements keep refining

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:32.580
which signals inside the brain

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.160
and environment actually move health outcomes.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:36.680
Before we go,

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.320
keep an eye on how the new computational framework

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.520
for linking personal genome sequences

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.560
to gene function

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.080
performs when applied to large biobank data sets.

